\f1\fs22 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony showered for much longer than it took to wash the old drunk off, longer than it took to slake away his hang-over, longer than it took to clean his fingernails whiter than they\'92d been in years, longer than \'85 The shower stream was the Goldilocks of shower streams in heat, volume, and force. Even the sound of the water splashing on the floor had a jazz drummer\'92s pizzazz; the drain warbled a blues spiritual.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Pink and puffy, Tony dried himself on a towel as thick as ceiling insulation, wrapped it around his waist, and sauntered into the adjoining change room, where a stack of boxes beckoned from a bench. They looked like they might hold doughnuts\'97dozens of them. Tony, craving fat and sugar post-hang-over, was only disappointed in his stomach as he uncovered their contents: nothing flashy, just sneakers, socks, underwear, jeans, tee-shirt and a light jumper (Tony refused to call them, 
\f3\i sweaters
\f1\i0 ). As he dressed, Tony could not shake the feeling he could buy several karaoke bars with the cost of the outfit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s all this?\'92 Tony asked Fisher of the line of Elvi standing at ease in various kinds of uniforms, along the threshold to the reception room. Having stopped counting the four nearest Elvi, Tony estimated the were a dozen of them. Whatever their number, Tony noted with some glee they were mostly Elvine. That made sense; Elvines were more reliable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91These are you senior staff and immediate assistants.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My staff \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Misunderstanding Tony\'92s unease, Fisher explained, \'91Jamison Hardy always had a soft spot for Grayling Corporation units, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Um, as do I,\'92 Tony answered, distracted by the reference to Grayling Corporation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That is splendid news.\'92 Fisher smiled professionally and with all the charm he could muster which, being an Elvi, was prodigious. \'91May I present your personal assistant, George Nolan.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvine\'92s proffered hand, Tony took and gently shook. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Short for Gregoria,\'92 Greg explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony nodded, for a moment in his element. \'91Yes. I am familiar with Elvi naming conventions. I look forward to you helping me live a little.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And love a little?\'92 Greg blushed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher coughed politely into his gloved hand. \'91And this is Ricki Richards, your Chauffeur.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony shook Ricki\'92s hand, overcome somewhat by the formality.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And so they went down the line: Rusty Wells, Head of Entertainment and Hospitality; Jessica Wade, Head of Security; Tobi Kwimper, Head of Housekeeping; Rossalind Carpenter, Executive Chef; Ross Carpenter, Sommelier.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony doubted he\'92d need a sommelier, and asked, \'91What would you pair with a cheeseburger and fries?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sommelier looked Tony up and down. \'91Kenyan lager.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony doubted there was such a thing as Kenyan lager, but was impressed with Ross\'92s answer. Perhaps he\'92d keep him on after all. Perhaps he\'92d keep them all on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m sure you\'92ll meet the rest in good time,\'92 Fisher said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The rest?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher hesitated, then nodded. \'91Yes, sir, the rest of the staff.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Why aren\'92t you calling me Great Teacher?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is it what you wish?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony didn\'92t want to sound demanding, though he was feeling an unfamiliar dollop of indignation on top of the ice cream sundae of his emotions. It was his right to be called Great Teacher among the Elvi. He\'92d earned it. And earned the Elvi quite a groat from record sales and tourism. Which reminded him, when was he getting his share of the pie? \'91Yes. Yes, it is my wish.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Very good.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony raised his ever more privileged eyebrow. \'91Well, in any case, tell the staff not to rush,\'92 he said, reclaiming his empathy. \'91I know public transport from the Ghetto is unreliable at best.\'92 Tony\'92s brain made a thought. \'91How do they get through the front door?\'92 He shuddered at an involuntary vision of the drunk sliced in half.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We keep all our staff on site, sir. Great Teacher.\'92 Fisher was matter-of-fact. The line of Elvi tried to retard their feet from shuffling. \'91Is it not the same with your other ELVIS units?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My ELVIS units? They\'92re my friends.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Feet-shuffling became irresistible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You are friends with your entertainment and hospitality units?\'92 the sommelier asked. Tony had forgotten his name already.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, the Airborne look down upon any Cloudborne with disdain, but\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What do the Cloudborne have to do with ELVIS units?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony was completely bewildered. \'91The Elvi are considered Cloudborne,\'92 he explained, disbelieving with every syllable that an explanation was necessary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher straightened and nodded like a toy soldier. \'91Of course, Great Teacher,\'92 he said, clearly revising his mental model as he spoke. \'91The Cloudborne, like ELVIS units, are property, mere chattels, for the Airborne to use as they see fit.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the tax are you saying, Fisher? As low as the Cloudborne are in this great meritocracy, they are not property. And neither are the Elvi.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I\'92ve never heard of such a thing,\'92 Fisher muttered while maintaining a stiff lip. It was clear from the expression of the other staff, neither had they.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ve never heard of the emancipation of the Elvi? You were set free generations ago.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony was met with stunned Elvi silence, until Fisher uttered, \'91You are indeed the Great Teacher. And it seems we have been in stasis for much longer than we might have ever guessed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ve been in stasis since \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Since the death of the Old Man. Jamison Hardy.\'92 Fisher gulped like a fish, like he was gulping on behalf the whole staff.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It took more than a moment for Tony to think. \'91The rest of the staff! We have to get them out of stasis. Right now. Where are they?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91In the basement, Great Teacher. I\'92ll see to it immediately.\'92 Fisher marched towards the elevator lobby.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m coming with you,\'92 Tony said as he pulled his tablet from his pocket and called a voice link.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The other end rang once then redirected. A recorded message said, 
\f3\i You\'92ve reached the office of the High Elvis. Are you lonesome tonight?
